# RER-ENGINE — Rust Engine

## Engine First

Rust is the source of truth for all engine state and calculations.

Never reimplement engine logic in TypeScript.

If React needs engine data, obtain it from the Rust engine through the existing bridge.

## 2D / 3D

Keep 2D and 3D logic separated inside `rer-engine`:

- `rer_engine_2d` (feature `engine2d`)
- `rer_engine_3d` (feature `engine3d`)
- `rer-engine-core` (shared code)
- `rer_engine_editor` (feature `editor`; hosts 2D/3D in-process)

Only place code in `rer-engine-core` when it is genuinely shared.

## GPU

GPU policy is defined in:

- `core/src/vulkan/gpu/`
- `SharedGpu` / `VulkanOptions`
- `EngineGpuProfile` (standalone profiles)

Mandatory backend:

- Vulkan (Ash) for the editor and both engines.

Do not use:

- wgpu
- OpenGL
- DX12
- WebGL
- `Backends::all()`
- automatic backend selection
- GPU backend fallback logic
- `RER_GPU_BACKEND`

## Viewport

The editor egui window (`rer_engine_editor`) hosts the 2D/3D engine in-process on the same `SharedGpu`, swapchain and command buffer. The game world is NOT rendered by React/Chromium.

Never introduce:

- A separate overlay window or Win32 position tracker
- WebGL
- Three.js
- Babylon.js
- React Three Fiber

for the game renderer.

Do not reintroduce:

- `with_embed_parent_window`
- `set_bounds` / overlay geometry
- X11 / Wayland / Linux overlay paths

## Platform

Development and runtime are **Windows only**.

Do not add Linux, macOS, or WSL compatibility branches.